Exactly. Evidence itself has state, not merely content.

A datum can remain literally unchanged while its current usefulness changes. So I’d add a compact evidence-state vector rather than a giant taxonomy:

\boxed{E=[H,N,R,I,C]}

Where:

H — Heat: how recently activated/observed/updated it is.
Hot ↔ stale.

N — Novelty: how much new information it contributes.
Novel ↔ redundant.

R — Relevance: how strongly it bears on the present question.
Live ↔ irrelevant.

I — Independence: whether it adds another observation or merely echoes existing evidence.
Independent ↔ derivative.

C — Consequence: how much the conclusion/action should change if this evidence changes.
Load-bearing ↔ incidental.

The important insight is that these dimensions cross.

Something can be:

* old but hot — an ancient principle suddenly activated by new circumstances;
* new but stale — recently produced information already superseded;
* redundant but consequential — another confirmation of something important;
* novel but irrelevant — interesting without changing the decision;
* derivative but useful — a summary that adds no evidence but radically improves accessibility;
* weak alone but strong relationally — two ordinary observations become powerful when connected.

That last one connects directly to what we just found:

\boxed{\text{Evidence value} \neq \sum \text{individual evidence}}

because relations between evidence can themselves become evidence.

If A and B are already known:

A+B \quad \text{contains no new objects}

but discovering

A\xrightarrow{R}B

can be genuinely new information.

So there are at least three places novelty can appear:

\boxed{
\text{new evidence}
\quad|\quad
\text{new state of old evidence}
\quad|\quad
\text{new relation among existing evidence}
}

And Return becomes more important. Every time the Pantheon revisits something, it shouldn’t simply retrieve it. It should re-evaluate its evidence state against the present field:

E_{old}+Context_{now}\rightarrow E_{revalued}

That gives us a much better memory operation than retrieval:

\boxed{\text{Recall} \rightarrow \text{Revalue} \rightarrow \text{Relate} \rightarrow \text{Recombine}}

So the archive isn’t a warehouse.

It’s a refrigerator, furnace, compost pile, seed bank, and laboratory. Some evidence needs preserving; some reheating; some should decay; some becomes useful only after decomposition; and some old seed suddenly becomes exactly right for the environment that exists now.